page cmd_status {
  title = "vmlab status"
  chapter_header "vmlab status" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "4 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-20"
    version      = "vmlab 0.31"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab status` reports what every machine in the current
    [lab](guide_lab_file) is doing, its IP address, the state of each
    [segment](guide_networking), and any download in flight. It reads the lab
    daemon's status projection and never starts anything.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab status [OPTIONS]" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`-v`, `--verbose`" | "Add the raw power state, readiness, and each machine's kind-specific detail (template/hardware, image/health/last exit)." |
      | "`--json`" | "Print the lab daemon's status projection as JSON instead of the tables. A lab with no daemon prints `{\"lab\": \"<name>\", \"running\": false}`." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  h2 "What it prints"

  p <<DOC
    With no lab daemon running the verb prints `lab "<name>": not running`
    and exits 0. A full [vmlab down](cmd_down) reaps the daemon, so a lab
    that is down reports this. When the lab's name is registered from
    another directory, the lab running under it is that directory's, not
    this one's, and `status` refuses with the same `conflict` as `up`,
    naming the other directory (see
    [The lab name is already registered](apx_troubleshooting)). Otherwise it prints up to four sections, each omitted when
    empty.
  DOC

  h3 "Machines"

  p <<DOC
    One row per machine with the columns `NAME`, `KIND` (`vm` or
    `container`), `STATUS`, `IP` and `TEMPLATE/IMAGE`. `STATUS` is a label
    the daemon derives from the power state, readiness and health, so every
    surface words a machine the same way:
  DOC

  table {
    rows:
      | "Label" | "Meaning" |
      | "`starting`" | "The process is being launched, or a container is up but its entrypoint has not signalled ready." |
      | "`booting`" | "A VM is running but not ready yet: its guest agent has not answered, or a first-boot script or agent refresh is still running." |
      | "`running`" | "Running and ready." |
      | "`unhealthy`" | "A container is ready but its declared healthcheck is failing." |
      | "`stopping`" | "The stop ladder is in progress." |
      | "`suspended`" | "A VM's guest put itself to sleep (ACPI S3). QEMU is alive and holds its memory, but nothing in the guest answers, so agent commands are refused with `conflict`. [vmlab vm start](cmd_vm) wakes it; `prevent_sleep = true` on the VM keeps it from staying asleep." |
      | "`stopped`" | "Powered off." |
      | "`exited (N)`" | "A container's entrypoint exited with a non-zero code." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    `IP` is the first NIC with a lease, as reported by the guest agent, or
    `-` before the guest is ready. With `--verbose` a second line under each
    machine carries `state=`, `ready=` and `cached=`, plus
    `diverged=yes` on a machine whose agent was replaced by
    [vmlab machine repair-agent](cmd_machine) or by the agent refresh in
    [vmlab up](cmd_up). A VM on a template with variants then adds
    `variant=`, the variant its clone is backed on, including the default a
    reference without `#` picked. Every VM adds `arch`, `cpus`,
    `memory` and `agent`: the version of the agent running in the guest, as
    its last handshake reported it, or the version the template sealed
    before an agent has answered or while the VM is stopped. An agent that
    restarts inside the guest is handshaken again the next time vmlab
    reaches it, so the version follows the restart. A VM with a
    [gpu block](ref_vm) adds `host_gpu=`, the host GPU its
    last start picked, `qemu=` when that GPU ran its own `qemu_binary`, and
    `env=NAME=value,...`: every variable vmlab set in that QEMU's
    environment, the `VK_*` and `VMLAB_*` loader filter and the host GPU's
    `qemu_env`, by name with values in full. That is what `qemu.log`'s
    `vmlab: env:` line records, taken at the start, so a host config edited
    since does not change what a running VM reports. See
    [Host GPUs](guide_host). A container adds `health`, `exit` and `digest`.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    With `--json` the verb prints the daemon's status projection as it
    arrived, the same object [vmlab lab](cmd_lab) `info` renders: each
    machine carries its `kind`, its label, and the fields above under their
    own names (`host_gpu`, `qemu_binary`, and `qemu_env` as an object of
    `NAME: value`), each omitted where it has nothing to say.
  DOC

  h3 "Dev machines"

  p <<DOC
    Labs with `@dev` machines get a second table: `DEV`, `DEFAULT` (whether it
    is the lab's default dev machine), `WORKSPACE` (the host directory) and `GUEST WORKSPACE`.
    A halted workspace syncer prints its halt sentence under the machine's
    row, followed by `vmlab dev sync status <machine>` as the place to read
    more. See [Dev machines and the workspace syncer](guide_dev) and
    [vmlab dev](cmd_dev).
  DOC

  h3 "Segments"

  p <<DOC
    One row per lab segment: `SEGMENT`, `SUBNET`, `GATEWAY`, `NAT/DHCP` as
    `on/off` pairs, `DROPPED` (frames the switch shed on this segment; anything
    other than 0 means the fabric is losing frames under load) and `PEER`
    (the cross-host trunk target and whether it is `up` or `down`).
  DOC

  h3 "Downloads"

  p <<DOC
    While a template or image is downloading, a table of `MACHINE`, `PULLING`
    (`template` or `container`), `PERCENT` and `REFERENCE` shows what an `up`
    that looks stuck is waiting on. See [vmlab pull](cmd_pull).
  DOC

  h2 "Examples"

  code sh { source = "vmlab status" }

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
lab "ad-lab"

  NAME     KIND      STATUS   IP           TEMPLATE/IMAGE
  dc01     vm        running  10.10.0.10   x86_64/winsrv2022
  client01 vm        booting  -            x86_64/win11
  buildbox vm        stopped  -            x86_64/ubuntu

  SEGMENT SUBNET         GATEWAY    NAT/DHCP DROPPED    PEER
  corp    10.10.0.0/24   10.10.0.1  on/on    0          -
SRC
  }

  p "Show the raw state behind each label:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab status -v" }

  p "Check which render server a Venus VM's QEMU was started with:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab status --json | jq '.machines[] | select(.name == \"gl01\") | .qemu_env'" }

  h2 "Exit status"

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0, including when the lab is not running. A lab directory
    that cannot be found, or a daemon whose status reply this binary cannot
    read (a version mismatch between CLI and daemon), exits 1 (`failed`). A
    lab name registered from another directory exits 5 (`conflict`). A
    usage error exits 2.
  DOC
}
